# The annotations of the boot program ($6060-$F6D4: the boot menu and the help): the single source of truth for
# src/bootmenu/*.asm (tools/bootmenu_mkasm.py, kit/mkgame.py's pipeline). The format is kit/mkgame.py's, class
# Annotations, plus @const:
#
#   @module NAME ; title            the entries from here on go to NAME.asm, the title heads the file
#   @about a paragraph              the module file's header text, one paragraph a line
#   $ADDR c Name ; title            a routine
#   > a description line
#   $ADDR b Name [N] ; title        data (w words, t text, s space; N items a line)
#   @res KIND/NAME                  the entry is a resource: src/res/bootmenu/..., INCBIN (tools/bootmenu_res.py)
#   $ADDR Name ; comment            a label and comment inside an entry
#   @isub $ADDR INSTRUCTION         the instruction written so
#   @equ $ADDR Name [N] ; comment   a variable outside the image
#   @const NAME VALUE ; comment     an EQU for an address in another bank or a value that only looks like one
#
# The code is what tools/bootmenu_sim.py ran in work/stage3/bootmenu/{tour,extra,nofire} (the boot menu, every help
# page, keys 1, 2 and 3, the three control sets), widened by mkgame's walk; frames there count from the boot
# program's entry (ane_sim frame 1473).

@equ $4000 Screen 6912 ; the screen: ScreenDepack's output, the printer's and the pointer's
@equ $5B00 Im2Table 257 ; the IM 2 vector table BootMenu fills with $60 (I = $5B: every vector reads $6060)
@const PAGE_PORT $7FFD ; the 128's paging port
@const AY_SELECT $FFFD ; the AY's register select (its data port is $BFFD: B = $BF)
@const ROM_RET $0052 ; a RET in the 48 BASIC ROM: CALL ROM_RET leaves the caller's address under SP
@const ROM_HL_ALT $2758 ; the value the BASIC ROM keeps in HL' (the depacker leaves it there)
@const BANK_WINDOW $C000 ; the start of the paged bank: the entry of the block or player that bank holds
@const PLAYER_PLAY $C005 ; bank 1: the boot tune player's play-one-frame entry (Pt3PlayEntry, src/music/pt3-player.asm)
@const STARTER $9C40 ; where the game starter's block runs (it unpacks itself there)
@const STARTER_LENGTH $0E09 ; the game starter's HRUM block at bank 7 $C000
@const GAME_BLOCK $6731 ; the game's HRUM block: copied here from bank 4 and bank 3, run with JP
@const GAME_HEAD_LENGTH $4000 ; the game block's first part, bank 4 $C000-$FFFF
@const GAME_TAIL $E79E ; bank 3: the game block's last part (behind Dead End)
@const GAME_TAIL_AT $A731 ; GAME_BLOCK + GAME_HEAD_LENGTH: where the last part goes
@const GAME_TAIL_LENGTH $0392 ; 914 bytes
@const MUSIC_BLOCK $EB30 ; bank 3: the game music's HRUM block, for bank 1 $C000
@const MUSIC_BLOCK_LENGTH $0F98
@const DISK_BLOCK $F637 ; bank 6: the disk module's HRUM block, for bank 4 $C000
@const DISK_BLOCK_LENGTH $08B1
@const SAMPLE $CE09 ; bank 7: the sample boot menu key 3 plays (after the starter's block)
@const SAMPLE_LENGTH $31F6 ; 12790 bytes, one a sample
@const DEPACK_GETBIT_ORG $62D5 ; GetBit where the authors assembled the depacker ($6203 + $D2): ScreenDepack writes the real address over these CALL operands before it runs

@module boot ; the entry, the interrupt, paging, the game's start, the sample
@about The boot program is block 5 of the disk (HRUM, src/sfx/blocks.asm Block05): UltraXOR's reader unpacks it to $6060-$F6D4 with bank 0 at $C000 and jumps to Start ($61A8) with SP = $6060. It holds the boot menu ("1-Game 2-Help 3-NOT used"), the help program (seven items, 28 pages of pictures and text) and the code that starts the game. Every other bank is loaded by then: bank 1 holds the boot tune (PT 3 Player v1.0 by Nick of GDC with X-Man's TOMY: src/music, aneS), bank 7 the game starter's block and a sample, banks 3, 4 and 6 the game's packed blocks.
@about Start plays the tune's init, unpacks bank 7's image and goes to BootMenu (menus.asm), which installs BootInterrupt as the IM 2 handler: from then on the tune plays one frame an interrupt, paging bank 1 in and out ($11 / $10 through SetPage), until key 1. Key 1 leaves through LeaveForGame (print.asm) to StartGame: the starter from bank 7, the game's blocks put together and unpacked, JP to the game's block. Key 3 ("NOT used") plays a 1.1 s sample from bank 7 through the AY's three volume registers (PlaySample).
@about Page values: $10 = bank 0 at $C000, the 48 BASIC ROM; $11 bank 1, $13 bank 3, $14 bank 4, $16 bank 6, $17 bank 7. The stack is the 284 bytes under Start ($608C-$61A7).
$6060 c BootInterrupt ; the IM 2 handler of the boot menu and the help: one frame of the boot tune
> Reached through Im2Table (every vector $6060, I = $5B) once a frame from BootMenu on. Keeps every register (both sets, IX, IY), pages bank 1 in, calls the player's play entry (PLAYER_PLAY, $C005 in bank 1), pages bank 0 back. The help's typing and pointer run under it; PlaySample turns interrupts off, LeaveForGame changes to IM 1.
$6060 ; nothing nests: the player is not reentrant
$6065 ; IX and IY too: the player uses them
$6069 ; and the second set
$606F ; bank 1 at $C000 (the tune and its player), the 48 ROM
$6074 ; one frame of the tune
@isub $6074 CALL PLAYER_PLAY
$6077 ; bank 0 back
$608A ; interrupts on for the next frame
$608C s Stack ; the boot program's stack, $608C-$61A7 (SP starts at Start)
> 284 zero bytes the image carries; Start and HelpMenu set SP = $61A8, the stack grows down into them.
$61A8 c Start ; the entry: the tune's init, bank 7's image unpacked, the boot menu
> Jumped to by the block's HRUM set-up (JP $61A8, interrupts off). Calls bank 1's $C000 (the player's init: the boot tune), then bank 7's $C000: bank 7's image is still packed there (BootBank7, src/sfx/blocks.asm) and unpacks in place with its depacker at $5B00, returning with interrupts off. Then BootMenu.
@isub $61A9 LD SP,Stack+284
$61A9 ; the stack under Start
$61AC ; bank 1
$61B1 ; the boot tune's init
$61B4 ; bank 7
$61B9 ; bank 7's image unpacks itself in place
$61BC ; bank 0
@isub $61B1 CALL BANK_WINDOW
@isub $61B9 CALL BANK_WINDOW
$61C4 c StartGame ; key 1: the game starter, the game's blocks put together and unpacked, the game
> Reached from LeaveForGame (IM 1 already). Copies the game starter's HRUM block (STARTER_LENGTH bytes from bank 7 $C000) to $9C40 and calls it: it unpacks over itself and runs the starter (the screen then shows "Eternity Industry": tour frame 11700). Then the game's packed block is put together at $6731: 16 K from bank 4 and its last 914 bytes from bank 3 $E79E; the game music's block goes from bank 3 to bank 1 $C000 and the disk module's from bank 6 to bank 4 $C000 (CopyBank, a byte at a time between two banks). Each of the three blocks at $C000 of banks 1, 3 (Dead End, loaded there) and 4 is then run: each HRUM block unpacks in place and returns. Last JP to the game's block at $6731, which unpacks the game over this program and starts it.
@isub $61C5 LD SP,Stack+284
@isub $61CD LD HL,BANK_WINDOW
@isub $61D0 LD DE,STARTER
@isub $61D3 LD BC,STARTER_LENGTH
@isub $61DD CALL STARTER
@isub $61EB LD HL,BANK_WINDOW
@isub $61EE LD DE,GAME_BLOCK
@isub $61F1 LD BC,GAME_HEAD_LENGTH
@isub $61FB LD HL,GAME_TAIL
@isub $61FE LD DE,GAME_TAIL_AT
@isub $6201 LD BC,GAME_TAIL_LENGTH
@isub $6206 LD HL,MUSIC_BLOCK
@isub $6209 LD DE,BANK_WINDOW
@isub $620C LD BC,MUSIC_BLOCK_LENGTH
@isub $6217 LD HL,DISK_BLOCK
@isub $621A LD DE,BANK_WINDOW
@isub $621D LD BC,DISK_BLOCK_LENGTH
@isub $622E CALL BANK_WINDOW
@isub $6236 CALL BANK_WINDOW
@isub $623E CALL BANK_WINDOW
@isub $6246 JP GAME_BLOCK
$61C8 ; bank 7: the starter's block at its $C000
$61D6 ; to $9C40, over the help's pictures
$61D8 ; bank 0
$61DD ; the starter unpacks itself and runs; it returns here
$61E0 ; 50 turns with interrupts on: a pending interrupt is taken
$61E6 ; bank 4: the game block's first 16 K
$61F6 ; bank 3: the game block's last part, behind Dead End
$620F ; from bank 3 (A = $13)
$6211 ; to bank 1
$6214 ; the game music's block to bank 1 $C000
$6220 ; from bank 6
$6223 ; to bank 4
$6226 ; the disk module's block to bank 4 $C000
$6229 ; bank 1
$622E ; the game music unpacks in place
$6231 ; bank 3
$6236 ; Dead End unpacks in place
$6239 ; bank 4
$623E ; the disk module unpacks in place
$6241 ; bank 0 for the game
$6246 ; the game's block unpacks the game and starts it; nothing returns
$6249 c SetPage ; $7FFD = A
> The boot program's only paging: every bank switch goes through here (BootInterrupt twice a frame). Keeps BC. The game starter calls it too (docs/03-build-and-load.md).
@isub $624A LD BC,PAGE_PORT
$6251 c CopyBank ; copy BC bytes from (HL) in page IXL to (DE) in page IXH
> One byte a turn: page IXL in, read, page IXH in, write. Used by StartGame for the blocks that go from one bank's $C000 window to another's. Leaves the last page (IXH) in.
$6256 ; the byte from the source bank
$625A ; the destination bank
$6267 c ClearScreen ; zero $5AFF down to the first byte that will not take a zero, then wait for a frame
> HL from $5B00 down: each byte is written 0 and read back; the loop ends at the first byte that reads non-zero, the ROM's $3FFF (writes to the ROM do nothing). So it clears the screen and the attributes ($4000-$5AFF) without a count. Then EI; HALT: the next frame (BootInterrupt plays the tune). Called before every picture is drawn.
@isub $6268 LD HL,Screen+6912
$6268 ; from the attributes' end down
$626B ; the next byte down
$626D ; reads back 0 while it is RAM
$6270 ; the ROM's $3FFF stopped it
$6273 c PlaySample ; boot menu key 3 ("NOT used"): 12790 samples from bank 7 through the AY's volumes
> Interrupts off (the tune stops), SilenceAy, bank 7. Each byte of SAMPLE ($CE09-$FFFE in bank 7) is written as it is to the AY's volume registers 8, 9 and 10 (the three channels together as one DAC), then a short wait (B = 8): 307 T a sample, 11.4 kHz on a Pentagon, 1.1 s. Then SilenceAy, bank 0, EI. The menu's "3-NOT used" hides it; extra frame 50 plays it.
@isub $627C LD HL,SAMPLE
@isub $627F LD DE,SAMPLE_LENGTH
@isub $6284 LD BC,AY_SELECT
$6277 ; bank 7
$6282 ; the sample
$6287 ; register 8: channel A's volume
$628C ; B = $BF: the data port $BFFD
$6293 ; register 9: channel B's volume
$629F ; register 10: channel C's volume
$62A8 ; the wait between samples
$62B2 ; the sound off again
$62B5 ; bank 0
$62BC c SilenceAy ; the AY's volumes 8-10 to 0, its mixer (register 7) to $FF
> Used by PlaySample before and after the sample.
@isub $62C2 LD BC,AY_SELECT
@isub $62D1 LD BC,AY_SELECT
$62BC ; three registers from 8
$62C7 ; volume 0
$62D4 ; register 7, the mixer
$62D8 ; B = $FF: every tone and noise off

@module helppic ; the picture of help item 1
@about The first of the fifteen Hrust 1 streams: the picture of "Описание игры" (help item 1), held before the depacker. The others are in pictures.asm. Each is a whole screen packed by Hrust 1 (tools/bootmenu_hrust.py has the format; the PNG in src/res/bootmenu/pictures). After every stream one byte nothing reads.
$62DE b DescriptionPicture ; help item 1 "Описание игры": a planet over a figure by a craft (Hrust 1)
> Drawn by HelpDescription for each of its 5 pages.
@res pictures/description.hrust
$68C4 b DescriptionPictureSpare ; a byte after the stream: never read

@module depack ; the screen depacker (Hrust 1)
@about ScreenDepack unpacks a Hrust 1 stream (Dmitry Pyankov's packer, the depacker without its header) from HL into the screen at $4000. It is position-independent: CALL ROM_RET leaves its own address under SP, and it writes the address of GetBit over the operands of its eleven CALLs (RelocTable: the distances between them) before it starts; the image holds the operands as the authors assembled them (DEPACK_GETBIT_ORG).
@about The stream: bits MSB first from 16-bit little-endian words, each fetched when the previous one's last bit is taken; a 0 bit is a literal byte, a 1 a match: the length from 2-4 more bits (2, 3, 4-5, 6-9) or a byte (10-263, $FE: a word, $FF: the end), then the distance: a 1 bit and a byte (1-256), or a 0, a prefix of 3-6 bits for the high byte (1-31) and a byte. A length-2 match has only the byte. tools/bootmenu_hrust.py packs as the authors' packer did: the longest match within 8192 bytes, the nearest of that length, unused bits of the last word set (a whole word fetched after the end code: 0).
$68C5 c ScreenDepack ; unpack the Hrust 1 stream at HL into the screen
> In: HL the stream. Uses both register sets: the main set holds the output (DE) and the match length (BC); the second the bit buffer (HL') and its count (B'). Called by BootMenu, HelpMenu and the help items; ClearScreen first, so the picture appears line by line in its own order (half a frame). Returns with HL' = ROM_HL_ALT for the ROM.
@isub $68C5 CALL ROM_RET
@isub $68CB LD IX,RelocTable+11-DepackHere
@isub $68D2 LD HL,GetBit-DepackHere
@isub $68EE CALL DEPACK_GETBIT_ORG
@isub $68FC CALL DEPACK_GETBIT_ORG
@isub $6903 CALL DEPACK_GETBIT_ORG
@isub $6906 CALL DEPACK_GETBIT_ORG
@isub $690E CALL DEPACK_GETBIT_ORG
@isub $6915 CALL DEPACK_GETBIT_ORG
@isub $6966 CALL DEPACK_GETBIT_ORG
@isub $696F CALL DEPACK_GETBIT_ORG
@isub $697E CALL DEPACK_GETBIT_ORG
@isub $6988 CALL DEPACK_GETBIT_ORG
@isub $6993 CALL DEPACK_GETBIT_ORG
@isub $692F LD HL,ROM_HL_ALT
@isub $68E8 LD DE,Screen
$68C5 ; a RET in the ROM: the return address, DepackHere, stays just under SP
$68C8 DepackHere ; SP back onto it
$68CA ; DE = DepackHere: where this copy of the depacker runs
$68CF ; IX = the end of RelocTable
$68D5 ; B = 0 (the main set's B stays 0: the high byte of a short distance, of a byte length)
$68D7 ; DE = GetBit, HL = DepackHere
$68D8 ; eleven CALL operands
$68DA PatchCalls ; the next distance, from the table's end
$68DF ; HL = the next CALL's operand
$68E0 ; = GetBit
$68E6 ; IX = the stream
$68E8 ; the output: the screen
$68EB ; the bit set: HL' the word, B' its bits left
$68EC ; the first GetBit only fetches the first word
$68F3 Literal ; a 0 bit: the next byte of the stream to the output
$68FC NextToken ; the next bit into the carry: 0 a literal, 1 a match
$6902 ; a match: A = 0, then two bits of its length code
$690A ; A = 0-3 again, Z when 0 (code 00: length 3)
$690E ; 01, 10, 11: a third bit (A = 2-7)
$6915 ; 11x: a fourth (A = 12-15)
$6918 LengthCode ; the main set; H = 0 for a distance of one byte
$691A ; 010: length 2 and only a distance byte (1-256)
$6921 ; 00: length 3
$6923 ; 011: a length byte follows
$6927 ; $FF: the end
$692F ; HL' as the ROM keeps it (the bit buffer is done)
$6934 ; $FE: a word length follows
$6937 ; else the byte + 10
$693B ; past 255 (bytes $F6-$FD): B = 1 (no stream here has one)
$693E ShortDistance ; the main set, H = 0
$693F CopyMatch ; L = the distance byte, H = its high part: HL = DE - HL - 1
$694B ; the match, BC bytes
$6950 WordLength ; the length as a little-endian word
$695C ; 100, 101: length 4 or 5
$6960 ; 1100-1111 (12-15): length 6-9
$6962 ; 00: 0 + 3
$6964 SetLength ; C = the length (B: 0, or 1 past 255)
$6965 ReadDistance ; the bit set: a 1 bit is a distance of one byte
$696C ; else the high byte's prefix: 3 bits first
$696F HighBits
$6975 ; 000, 001: high 1, 2
$697A SetHigh ; the main set: H = the distance's high byte, the byte follows in the stream
$697E ; 010x, 011x (4-7): high 3-6
$6988 ; 1xxxx below 10111 (16-22): high 7-13
$6993 ; 10111x-11111x (46-63): high 14-31
$699A c GetBit ; the next bit into A's bit 0 (and the carry out of A's bit 7)
> In the second set: HL the bit buffer, B the bits left in it. Shifts HL left (the carry is the bit), counts B down and fetches the next word from the stream (IX) when the 16th bit is taken; RLA puts the bit into A. ScreenDepack calls it through the operands it patched.
$699B ; bits left: done
$699D ; the next word, low byte first
$69A9 ; the bit into A
$69AB b RelocTable 11 ; the distances between GetBit's CALL operands, read from the end
> ScreenDepack starts at DepackHere + $27 (the first CALL's operand, $68EF) and adds each byte from the table's end down: $68EF, $68FD, $6904, $6907, $690F, $6916, $6967, $6970, $697F, $6989, $6994.

@module pictures ; the help's pictures and the boot menu's (Hrust 1 streams)
@about Fourteen whole screens packed by Hrust 1 (ScreenDepack, depack.asm), each followed by one byte nothing reads. src/res/bootmenu/pictures holds them as PNG; the build packs them back to these bytes (tools/bootmenu_hrust.py). The eight of help item 3 (HelpComponents) show one robot part a page; the others one help item's pages each, the help menu and the boot menu.
