@res pictures/boot-menu.hrust
$AA68 b BootMenuPictureSpare ; never read

@module menus ; the boot menu, the help menu, the pointer, the controls
@about BootMenu installs the interrupt and shows its picture; its loop reads the keys 1-3 (half row $F7FE): 1 LeaveForGame (print.asm), 2 HelpMenu, 3 PlaySample (boot.asm). The help menu is a picture with seven empty bars; HelpMenu prints the item names into them at once (TypeAtOnce = 1) and runs a loop a frame: the arrow pointer is drawn (DrawPointer), the controls read (ReadControls), the pointer moved (MovePointer), a HALT, the pointer erased (ErasePointer); without fire the bar under the pointer is lit (FindItem, HighlightItem: ink and paper of its cells swapped), with fire RunItem jumps to the lit bar's routine through ItemRoutines.
@about The pointer is a 16-line, 8-pixel arrow (PointerSprite, mask and image) drawn over the screen with the 16 x 2 bytes under it saved (PointerUnder); its place is Pointer (x, y in pixels, the hot spot one pixel right and down of the arrow's corner), kept within PointerLimits. It starts at x 195, y 55 (BootMenu) and stays where it was when a help item returns. It moves Speed pixels a frame: 1 at first, one more every 8 frames the same direction is held, at most 10; a change of the controls starts again at 1.
@about The controls (Controls: bit 0 right, 1 left, 2 down, 3 up, 4 fire) are Q A O P and SPACE together with either the keys 6 7 8 9 0 (the Sinclair joystick: left, right, down, up, fire) or, with CAPS SHIFT held, the cursor keys 5 6 7 8 and 0. There is no Kempston read. The item's hit boxes (ItemBoxes) are the bars' text cells: columns 9-22 of rows 7, 9, ... 19.
$AA69 c BootMenu ; the boot menu: the tune's init again, the IM 2 interrupt, then ShowBootMenu
> From Start. Calls bank 1's $C000 (the player's init: the boot tune from its start), fills Im2Table with $60 (257 bytes), I = $5B, IM 2, EI and a HALT: BootInterrupt plays from the next frame on. Falls into ShowBootMenu.
@isub $AA6E CALL BANK_WINDOW
$AA69 ; bank 1
$AA6E ; the tune from its start
$AA71 ; bank 0
@isub $AA7E LD BC,256
$AA7C ; every byte $60: whatever the data bus holds, the vector read is $6060
$AA83 ; I = $5B
$AA8A ; the first BootInterrupt
$AA8B c ShowBootMenu ; the boot menu's picture, the pointer's start, the key loop
> Also help item 7 ("Выход", through ItemRoutines). Border black, ClearScreen, BootMenuPicture, Pointer = x 195, y 55 (where the help menu will show it). The loop reads the half row 1-5: 1 LeaveForGame, 2 HelpMenu, 3 PlaySample and on; nothing waits for a key's release, so key 3 held plays the sample again and again.
@isub $AA97 LD DE,55*256+195
$AA8C ; border black
$AA97 ; y 55, x 195
$AA9E BootMenuKeys ; the half row 1-5
$AAA3 ; key 1: the game
$AAA7 ; key 2: the help
$AAAB ; key 3: the sample
$AAB1 c HelpMenu ; the help menu: its picture, the items printed into the bars, the pointer loop
> Reached from ShowBootMenu (key 2), from every help item's end, from SPACE while a page types (PrintString) or after it (WaitPageKey): SP is set again, so nothing left on the stack matters. Border black, ClearScreen, HelpMenuPicture; TypeAtOnce = 1, the seven names and the copyright line printed at their places (row, column in 4-pixel cells: 7,25 9,22 11,19 13,20 15,24 17,28 19,29 and 22,8: each name centred on its bar); ResetPointerSpeed, LitItem = none. Then a frame a turn: DrawPointer, ReadControls, MovePointer, HALT; with fire RunItem (it does not come back when a bar is lit), else FindItem and HighlightItem; ErasePointer. The pointer is visible from the frame's start to its HALT, so the HALT shows it.
@isub $AAB1 LD SP,Stack+284
@isub $AAC5 LD DE,7*256+25
@isub $AAD2 LD DE,9*256+22
@isub $AADF LD DE,11*256+19
@isub $AAEC LD DE,13*256+20
@isub $AAF9 LD DE,15*256+24
@isub $AB06 LD DE,17*256+28
@isub $AB13 LD DE,19*256+29
@isub $AB20 LD DE,22*256+8
$AAB1 ; the stack from its top: the help menu is a fresh start
$AAB5 ; border black
$AAC0 ; the names print at once
$AAC5 ; row 7, column 25
$AAD2 ; row 9, column 22
$AADF ; row 11, column 19
$AAEC ; row 13, column 20
$AAF9 ; row 15, column 24
$AB06 ; row 17, column 28
$AB13 ; row 19, column 29
$AB20 ; the copyright line, row 22, column 8
$AB2D ; the pointer at 1 pixel a frame
$AB30 ; no bar lit
$AB35 HelpLoop
$AB3E ; one frame with the pointer shown
$AB42 ; fire?
$AB46 ; no: the bar under the pointer, lit
$AB4C ; the screen as it was for the next frame's pointer
$AB51 HelpFire ; RunItem does not come back when a bar is lit
$AB59 c FindItem ; the item under the pointer: A = 1-7, or $FF (CurrentItem then counts 8)
> The pointer's x and y divided by 8 give the cell; each ItemBoxes record (column, row, width) is tried: the cell's column within the box's and its row equal. CurrentItem counts the records from 1.
$AB5D ; CurrentItem = 1
$AB62 ; L = x, H = y
$AB65 ; H = y / 8: the cell's row
$AB6C ; L = x / 8: its column
$AB72 ; H = the column, L = the row
$AB74 FindItemNext ; the box's first column ($FF: no more boxes, A = $FF)
$AB7D ; left of the box
$AB84 ; A = its last column
$AB86 ; right of it
$AB88 ; its row
$AB8E ; A = the item
$AB92 FindItemSkip ; the next record
$AB97 ; CurrentItem + 1
$ABA0 c HighlightItem ; light the bar of item A (or none, $FF), the old one put back
> When A is LitItem nothing changes. Else the old lit bar (if any) is inverted back (InvertItem) and LitItem = A; the new one is inverted when it is a bar.
$ABA4 ; the same bar: nothing to do
$ABA6 ; none lit yet
$ABAA ; LitItem = the new one
$ABB0 ; the old bar back
$ABB3 InvertLit ; the new bar (none: done)
$ABD2 FirstLit ; LitItem = the new one
$ABB9 c InvertItem ; swap ink and paper over item A's bar
> IX = its ItemBoxes record; InvertCells at its column and row, its width cells.
$ABBF ; HL = (A - 1) * 3
$ABC2 ; IX = the record
$ABC8 ; H = the column, L = the row
$ABD8 c InvertCells ; swap ink and paper of (IX + 2) cells from column H, row L
> The attribute address $5800 + row * 32 + column, computed from H and L. The first cell's attribute, swapped, is written to all the cells; bright and flash are dropped (the bars have neither).
$ABD8 ; A = the column
$ABD9 ; L = row * 8 (rows 0-23 fit a byte)
$ABDC ; H = $16: HL * 4 = $5800 + row * 32
$ABE0 ; + the column
$ABE2 ; the first cell's attribute
$ABE4 ; the ink becomes the paper
$ABEE ; the paper becomes the ink
$ABF7 ; the width
$ABFA InvertCellsNext ; every cell of the bar
$ABFF c RunItem ; fire: jump to the lit item's routine, dropping the caller (nothing lit: return)
> The routine's address from ItemRoutines; PUSH; RET. The routines end with JP HelpMenu (item 7 is ShowBootMenu itself), which sets SP again.
$AC04 ; nothing lit: back to the loop
$AC05 ; HelpLoop's return address dropped
$AC0B ; the item's routine
$AC12 ; into it
$AC14 b ItemBoxes 3 ; the seven bars' hit boxes: column, row, width in 8-pixel cells
> Columns 9-22 (14 cells) of rows 7, 9, 11, 13, 15, 17, 19; $FF ends the table.
$AC29 ; the end
$AC2A w ItemRoutines 1 ; the seven items' routines, in the menu's order
> HelpDescription, HelpProduction, HelpComponents, HelpProgramming, HelpFeatures, HelpCredits, ShowBootMenu ("Выход").
$AC38 b CurrentItem ; FindItem's count: the item under the pointer, 8 past the last
$AC39 b LitItem ; the lit bar's item, $FF none (RunItem's choice)
$AC3A c WaitFrames ; ten HALTs: no code calls it
> A routine left in the image: LD B,10, HALT, DJNZ, RET. Not referenced (the bytes $3A,$AC appear nowhere) and never run.
$AC3C WaitFramesNext
$AC40 c ResetPointerSpeed ; Speed = SpeedStart: the pointer at 1 pixel a frame again
> Also reached from PointerAcceleration when the controls change or no direction is held.
@isub $AC46 LD BC,3
$AC4C c MovePointer ; move the pointer by the controls: x first, then y, Speed pixels, clamped
> Controls' bits: 0 right, 1 left, 2 down, 3 up. PointerAcceleration first. A direction counts only alone on its axis (B = C XOR (C >> 1 AND 5): right with left cancel, down with up). StepAxis moves x (DE = Pointer, HL = its limits), then y (Pointer + 1, the y limits).
$AC50 ; the speed for these controls
$AC54 ; C = the controls
$AC55 ; B bit 0: right XOR left, bit 2: down XOR up
$AC5A ; carry: x moves
$AC5B ; x's limits
$AC62 ; C bit 0 (right) adds, else (left) subtracts
$AC66 ; C bit 0 now: down
$AC6B ; carry (B bit 2): y moves
$AC6E ; y
$AC6F ; y's limits
$AC75 c PointerAcceleration ; A = the controls: speed up while the same controls are held
> LastInput = A. A change from the last frame (fire too), or no direction: ResetPointerSpeed. Else Speed's delay counts down; at 0 it is reloaded (8 frames) and, while the steps left (10 at the start) last, Speed's step grows by one: 1 pixel a frame, 2 after 8 frames, at most 10 after 72.
$AC75 ; C = the last frame's controls (B, PointerAddress's low byte, is not used)
$AC79 ; this frame's for the next
$AC7C ; changed: start slow again
$AC7F ; no direction: the same
$AC83 ; Speed's delay
$AC88 ; 8 frames on
$AC8D ; steps left
$AC8F ; none left: the fastest
$AC92 ; one pixel faster
$AC94 c StepAxis ; move one coordinate (DE) by Speed's step: C bit 0 set adds, clear subtracts; clamp to (HL) and (HL + 1)
> Subtracting below the minimum or past 0 gives the minimum; adding up to the maximum or past 255 gives the maximum.
@isub $AC96 LD A,(Speed+2)
$AC95 ; carry: add (right, down)
$AC96 ; the step
$AC9D ; subtract (left, up)
$AC9E ; past 0
$ACA1 ; below the minimum
$ACA5 StepUp ; add
$ACA6 ; HL on the maximum
$ACA7 ; past 255
$ACAA ; below the maximum: done
$ACAC ClampAxis ; the limit
$ACAF c DrawPointer ; save the 16 x 2 bytes under the pointer and draw it (mask and image) at Pointer
> The corner is one pixel left and up of Pointer (unless at 0). PointerAddress = the screen byte of the corner (where PointerUnder goes back); nothing is drawn when the corner is below the pixel area ($5800), and the drawing stops there. Each sprite line (PointerSprite: mask, image) is shifted to the pixel and laid over two screen bytes (AND the mask, OR the image); at column 31 only the first byte. The save takes 16 lines even past the pixel area (reads only).
$ACAF ; E = x, D = y
$ACB3 ; y - 1 unless 0
$ACB8 ; x - 1 unless 0
$ACBD ; HL = the screen address of the corner (x, y)
$ACD3 ; for ErasePointer
$ACD6 ; the corner below the pixel area: nothing drawn
$ACDA ; the bytes under the pointer
$ACDD ; B = 16 lines (C = $FF: the 32 LDIs leave B alone)
$ACE0 SaveUnder ; two bytes a line
$ACEC ; the shift: 8 - (x - 1) & 7, 1-8 (9 at x 0, which PointerLimits keeps out)
$ACFA ; the sprite
$ACFD ; 16 lines
$ACFF DrawLine ; HL = the sprite line, DE = the screen
$AD03 ; D:E = $FF, the mask; H:L = 0, the image
$AD0A ShiftLine ; SLL (undocumented: a 1 shifted in): the mask left into D, the image into H
$AD11 ; B:C = the image's two bytes
$AD13 ; the left byte: AND the mask, OR the image
$AD18 ; past column 31 (L wraps to the next row): no right byte
$AD1E ; the right byte
$AD22 DrawLineNext
$AD2B ; past the pixel area: stop
$AD32 c ErasePointer ; put back the bytes DrawPointer saved; PointerAddress then points past the pixels
> Stops at the pixel area's end as DrawPointer did. PointerAddress = $58xx afterwards, so a second erase restores nothing.
$AD3A RestoreLine ; past the pixel area: stop
$AD4B ; a second erase stops at once
$AD51 c NextLineDown ; HL = the screen address one pixel line down
> Past the screen's last line H becomes $58: the callers stop there.
$AD60 b Pointer 2 ; the pointer's x, y in pixels (192, 55 in the image; ShowBootMenu sets 195, 55)
$AD62 b PointerLimits 2 ; x 1-255, y 9-182
$AD66 b SpeedStart 3 ; Speed's start: the delay 8, the steps 10, the step 1 pixel
$AD69 b Speed 3 ; the delay counter, the steps left, the step in pixels
$AD6C b LastInput ; the controls of the last frame
$AD6D w PointerAddress ; the screen byte of the pointer's corner (where PointerUnder goes back)
$AD6F b PointerSprite 2 ; the arrow: 16 lines of mask (AND), image (OR)
@res sprites/pointer.bin
$AD8F b PointerUnder 2 ; the 16 x 2 screen bytes under the pointer
$ADAF b PointerSpare ; 4 bytes nothing uses
$ADB3 c ReadControls ; Controls = Q A O P SPACE with 6 7 8 9 0, or with the cursor keys 5 6 7 8 0 when CAPS SHIFT is held
> Bit 0 right, 1 left, 2 down, 3 up, 4 fire. Each reader shifts five keys into C, inverts them (1 = pressed) and stores Controls itself (the shared tail at StoreControls); the last store is the OR of both readers.
$ADB5 ; the half row CAPS SHIFT-V
$ADB9 ; CAPS SHIFT in the carry
$ADBA ; held: the cursor keys
$ADBC ; else the Sinclair joystick's keys
$ADBF MergeControls
$ADC1 ; and Q A O P SPACE
$ADC6 StoreControls
$ADCC CursorControls
$ADD1 c ReadQaop ; SPACE, Q, A, O, P into C's low five bits (fire, up, down, left, right)
$ADD1 ; SPACE
$ADD8 ; Q
$ADDF ; A
$ADE6 ; P (bit 0) and O (bit 1)
$ADEA LeftRight ; A's bit 1 (left), then its bit 0 (right)
$ADF0 LastBit
$ADF2 ; 1 = pressed
$ADF6 c ReadCursorKeys ; with CAPS SHIFT: 0 fire, 7 up, 6 down, 5 left, 8 right
$ADF6 ; 0 9 8 7 6
$ADFA ; 0: fire
$ADFD ; 9 not used; 8 (right) into the carry, kept for last
$AE00 ; 7: up
$AE03 ; 6: down
$AE06 ; 5: left, on the next half row
$AE0C ; the carry = 5 not pressed
$AE10 ; 8: right
$AE13 c ReadSinclairJoystick ; 0 fire, 9 up, 8 down, 6 left, 7 right (the Sinclair joystick on the keys 6-0)
$AE13 ; 0 9 8 7 6
$AE17 ; 0, 9, 8
$AE19 SinclairNext
$AE1E ; then 6 and 7 as O and P
$AE20 b Controls ; this frame's controls: bit 0 right, 1 left, 2 down, 3 up, 4 fire

@module help ; the help items: pictures and typed pages
@about Each of the six help items (ItemRoutines) clears the screen, sets TypeAtOnce = 0 (the typewriter) and shows its pages: per page the picture is unpacked again (ScreenDepack), the text cursor set to row 1 (rows 5-9 for the parts of item 3), column 30, and ShowPage types the page's string into the right half (columns 30-62 of 64 four-pixel cells, 33 a line) and waits: ENTER or 0 the next page, SPACE the help menu (also while it types). After the last page JP HelpMenu. Items 3, 5 and 6 wait one more frame (HALT) between their pages.
@about Typing takes about a quarter of a frame a character (PrintString's delay): a full page of 759 characters about 200 frames. Items: 1 "Описание игры" 5 pages, 2 "Производство роботов" 4, 3 "Характеристика компонентов" 8 (a part and its picture a page: electronics, A-bomb, phasers, missiles, cannons, anti-gravs, tracks, bipods), 4 "Программирование роботов" 3, 5 "Особенности игры" 5, 6 "CREDITZ" 3. The texts are in texts.asm (src/res/bootmenu/text).
$AE21 c HelpCredits ; help item 6 "CREDITZ": CreditsPicture, 3 pages
$AE24 ; the typewriter
$AE28 ; page 1: the picture
$AE2E ; at row 1, column 30
$AE35 ; the text
$AE3B ; a frame between the pages (items 3, 5 and 6 only)
$AE67 c HelpFeatures ; help item 5 "Особенности игры": FeaturesPicture, 5 pages
$AED5 c ShowPage ; type the string at HL, then wait for ENTER / 0 (back) or SPACE (the help menu)
$AEDB c HelpDescription ; help item 1 "Описание игры": DescriptionPicture, 5 pages
$AF44 c HelpProduction ; help item 2 "Производство роботов": ProductionPicture, 4 pages
$AF9A c HelpProgramming ; help item 4 "Программирование роботов": ProgrammingPicture, 3 pages
$AFDD c HelpComponents ; help item 3 "Характеристика компонентов": a part a page, its picture each
> Electronics, A-bomb, phasers, missiles, cannons, anti-gravs, tracks, bipods; the text starts at row 5, 7, 9, 8, 9, 8, 8, 7 (under the part's name in the picture).

@isub $AE2E LD DE,1*256+30
@isub $AE42 LD DE,1*256+30
@isub $AE56 LD DE,1*256+30
@isub $AE74 LD DE,1*256+30
@isub $AE88 LD DE,1*256+30
@isub $AE9C LD DE,1*256+30
@isub $AEB0 LD DE,1*256+30
@isub $AEC4 LD DE,1*256+30
@isub $AEE8 LD DE,1*256+30
@isub $AEFB LD DE,1*256+30
@isub $AF0E LD DE,1*256+30
@isub $AF21 LD DE,1*256+30
@isub $AF34 LD DE,1*256+30
@isub $AF51 LD DE,1*256+30
@isub $AF64 LD DE,1*256+30
@isub $AF77 LD DE,1*256+30
@isub $AF8A LD DE,1*256+30
@isub $AFA7 LD DE,1*256+30
@isub $AFBA LD DE,1*256+30
@isub $AFCD LD DE,1*256+30
@isub $AFEA LD DE,5*256+30
@isub $AFFE LD DE,7*256+30
@isub $B012 LD DE,9*256+30
@isub $B026 LD DE,8*256+30
@isub $B03A LD DE,9*256+30
@isub $B04E LD DE,8*256+30
@isub $B062 LD DE,8*256+30
@isub $B076 LD DE,7*256+30

@module print ; the help's text printer, the page keys, the game key
@about PrintString prints a 0-terminated string at PrintAt (column 0-63 in 4-pixel half cells, row 0-23): code 1 switches to FontLatin, 2 to FontCyrillic (UseLatin / UseCyrillic patch the font base in PrintGlyph), every other byte is a glyph. PrintGlyph ORs the glyph's 7 lines (its bytes 1-7; byte 0 is the blank line above) into the left or right half of the screen byte (masks $F0 / $0F): text over a picture keeps the picture. Past column 62 the line goes on at column 30 of the next row; past row 23, row 0.
@about With TypeAtOnce = 0 (the pages) each glyph is followed by a delay (BC = $02BC: about 18000 T) and a look at SPACE, which drops the page for HelpMenu. With 1 (the help menu's names) neither.
$B086 c UseLatin ; code 1: the Latin font from here; A = the next byte, HL on it
@isub $B087 LD HL,FontLatin-$0100
$B087 ; glyph = code * 8 + FontLatin - $100
$B091 c UseCyrillic ; code 2: the Cyrillic font from here; A = the next byte, HL on it
@isub $B092 LD HL,FontCyrillic-$0100
$B09C c PrintGlyph ; print glyph A at PrintAt, then PrintAt on one column
> The glyph's address: A * 8 + the font base (GlyphFont's operand). The column wraps at 63 to 30 on the next row, the row at 24 to 0 (never on the help's pages). The screen byte from ScreenRows and the column / 2; the column's bit 0 picks the right half (mask $0F) or the left ($F0). Seven lines from the cell's second.
@isub $B0A0 LD DE,FontLatin-$0100
@isub $B0B1 LD A,(PrintAt+1)
@isub $B0BE LD A,(PrintAt+1)
$B09C ; HL = the code
$B09F ; B = 0 (PrintString's delay sets it again)
$B0A0 GlyphFont ; the font base: UseLatin / UseCyrillic write the operand
$B0A6 ; HL = the glyph
$B0A8 ; PrintAt: the column
$B0AC GlyphColumn ; C = the column
$B0AD ; past column 62
$B0B1 ; the next row
$B0B8 ; column 30 of it
$B0BC GlyphHere ; the column after this one
$B0C1 ; past row 23
$B0C5 ; row 0 (the help never gets there)
$B0C9 ; the row's screen address
$B0D2 ; the column's byte, its half in the carry
$B0DE ; the right half
$B0E0 ; the left half
$B0E2 ; the glyph's line 0 is not drawn: lines 1-7 to the cell's lines 1-7
$B0E4 ; OR the glyph's half into the screen
$B10D RightHalf ; the same with the low nibble
$B13A c PrintString ; print the 0-terminated string at HL: codes 1 / 2 the fonts, with TypeAtOnce 0 a delay a glyph and SPACE to the help menu
$B13A PrintNext
$B13C ; 0: the end
$B13E ; 1: Latin from here
$B143 ; 2: Cyrillic from here
$B149 ; the glyph
$B14C ; at once?
$B152 ; SPACE: the help menu (it sets the stack again)
@isub $B15A LD BC,700
$B15A ; the typewriter's delay: 700 turns of 26 T
$B15D TypeDelay
$B162 PrintSkip ; the next byte
$B166 PrintEnd ; the 0
$B167 b TypeAtOnce ; 1: PrintString prints at once (the help menu), 0: types (the pages)
$B168 c LeaveForGame ; boot menu key 1: the tune from its start, the screen cleared, IM 1, StartGame
> Calls bank 1's $C000 (the player's init: the tune from its start, its AY registers set), bank 0, ClearScreen (one more interrupt: a frame of the tune), I = $3F, IM 1, EI, JP StartGame.
@isub $B16E CALL BANK_WINDOW
$B169 ; bank 1
$B16E ; the tune's init: the tune back at its start
$B171 ; bank 0
$B176 ; one more frame under IM 2 (ClearScreen's HALT)
$B179 ; I as the ROM keeps it
$B17D ; IM 1: no more BootInterrupt
$B183 c WaitPageKey ; ENTER or 0: return (the next page); SPACE: the help menu
> A HALT a turn (the tune plays on).
$B183 ; ENTER
$B189 ; 0
$B18F ; SPACE
$B198 ; a frame
$B19B b PrintAt 2 ; the printer's column (0-63, 4-pixel cells) and row (0-23)
$B19D b FontLatin 8 ; the Latin font: 96 glyphs $20-$7F, 8 bytes each (line 0 blank), 4 pixels in both nibbles
@res fonts/latin.bin
$B49D b FontCyrillic 8 ; the Cyrillic font: the same codes, the authors' KOI-7-like layout (a = а, q = я, w = в ...)
@res fonts/cyrillic.bin
$B79D w ScreenRows 8 ; the screen address of each character row's first line, rows 0-23

@module texts ; the help menu's names and the 28 pages
@about The strings PrintString prints (print.asm), 0-terminated, each starting with its font code (1 Latin, 2 Cyrillic). src/res/bootmenu/text holds them as UTF-8 (tools/bootmenu_res.py: the glyphs of the font in force, the codes as ‹lat› / ‹rus›, the pages in lines of 33). A page's text is one string: the printer wraps it at column 62, so the authors padded the lines with spaces to 33.
$B7CD b MenuDescription ; "ОПИСАНИЕ ИГРЫ"
@res text/menu.bin
$B7DC b MenuProduction ; "ПРОИЗВОДСТВО РОБОТОВ"
@res text/menu.bin
$B7F2 b MenuComponents ; "ХАРАКТЕРИСТИКА КОМПОНЕНТОВ"
@res text/menu.bin
$B80E b MenuProgramming ; "ПРОГРАММИРОВАНИЕ РОБОТОВ"
@res text/menu.bin
$B828 b MenuFeatures ; "ОСОБЕННОСТИ ИГРЫ"
@res text/menu.bin
$B83A b MenuCredits ; "CREDITZ"
@res text/menu.bin
$B843 b MenuExit ; "ВЫХОД"
@res text/menu.bin
$B84A b MenuCopyright ; "(C) 1998 ETERNITY INDUSTRY. KOVROV COOL CITY."
@res text/menu.bin
$B879 b CreditsPage1 ; "TOTAL CREDITZ": the intro's and the help's authors
@res text/credits.bin
$BB30 b CreditsPage2 ; the intro's testers, the game's authors, the tape version's supplier
